{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Lifecycle · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Lifecycle</h1>
    <p>
        php-via provides hooks at every stage, from a worker's start to a tab's last cleanup. Use them to
        manage timers, presence tracking, background work and resource cleanup.
    </p>

    <h2 id="workers">Worker start and stop</h2>
    <p>
        <code>$app-&gt;onWorkerStart()</code> runs in every worker process when it starts, and
        <code>$app-&gt;onWorkerStop()</code> when it stops. Both callbacks get the worker's id. With
        <code>withWorkerNum(4)</code> they run four times, and again for a worker that restarts after a reload,
        a <code>max_request</code> recycle or a crash. Run work meant for one worker where
        <code>$workerId === 0</code>: worker 0 always exists and restarts under the same id.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->onWorkerStart(function (int $workerId) use ($app): void {
    if ($workerId === 0) {
        $app->setGlobalState('started_at', time());
    }
});

$app->onWorkerStop(function (int $workerId) use ($db): void {
    $db->close(); // end connections and long-lived coroutines here
});
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A stopping worker first waits up to half its stop budget for open SSE streams and
        <code>spawn()</code> tasks, then runs the stop callbacks. A callback cannot tell a reload from a stop.
    </p>

    <h2 id="connection-lifecycle">Connection lifecycle</h2>
    <p>Here's what happens for each tab:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Page load (GET)</strong>: the page handler runs, the initial HTML is rendered</li>
        <li><strong>SSE connect (GET /_sse)</strong>: the view renders again and <code>onClientConnect</code> fires</li>
        <li><strong>Actions</strong>: POST to <code>/_action/{name}</code>, the callback runs, patches stream back</li>
        <li><strong>SSE disconnect</strong>: <code>onClientDisconnect</code> fires at once</li>
        <li><strong>Cleanup (after a timeout)</strong>: <code>onCleanup</code> callbacks fire, timers are cancelled</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        If the browser reconnects within the cleanup delay (default: 5 seconds), the context is reused
        and cleanup is cancelled: you won't see a spurious disconnect/reconnect for normal page refreshes.
        Configure the delay with <code>Config::withContextTimeouts(cleanupDelayMs: ...)</code>. A longer delay
        tolerates flakier clients at the cost of holding idle contexts (and their in-memory view
        payloads) in memory longer under concurrent disconnects; pass <code>0</code> to clean up
        immediately with no reconnect grace period:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withContextTimeouts(cleanupDelayMs: 60_000); // 60 seconds
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>withContextTimeouts()</code> changes only the timers you name and keeps the others as they are.
    </p>

    <h3 id="without-stream">Contexts without a stream</h3>
    <p>
        A context whose SSE stream never connects (a crawler, a prefetch, a tab closed before it
        connected) is destroyed after the connect timeout, 30 seconds by default, and its
        <code>onCleanup</code> callbacks run. With several workers the same
        applies to a copy an action rebuilt on a worker the tab does not stream from; every action on it
        starts the timeout again. A tab that connects later is rebuilt by revival.
        <code>Config::withContextTimeouts(connectMs: 0)</code> keeps such contexts until the worker stops.
    </p>
    <p>
        An action that reaches a tab whose stream is down, because the stream dropped or because the
        action revived the context after the cleanup delay, keeps the context for the reconnect timeout,
        60 seconds by default, counted from the last action. Datastar waits at most 30 seconds between
        two reconnect attempts (<code>retryMaxWait</code>), so the patches the action queued are sent when
        the stream comes back. If you raise <code>retryMaxWait</code>, raise the timeout above it:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withContextTimeouts(reconnectMs: 60_000); // default; 0 uses the connect timeout
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Which timer frees a context without a stream:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Its stream never connected: the connect timeout (30&nbsp;s).</li>
        <li>Its stream dropped: the cleanup delay (5&nbsp;s).</li>
        <li>An action reached it while it had no stream: the reconnect timeout (60&nbsp;s) after the last action.</li>
        <li>An action rebuilt it on a worker the tab does not stream from: the connect timeout after the last action.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Queued patches live in the context. A context freed before its stream returns drops them, together
        with whatever its actions changed in TAB state. The tab that reconnects later is revived with the
        signal values it holds and a fresh render of its view, but a one-off patch such as an
        <code>execScript()</code> is gone.
    </p>

    <h3 id="reconnect">How a page reconnects</h3>
    <p>
        <code>via_head</code> opens the stream with <code>@get('/_sse')</code> as soon as Datastar starts, and
        again every 15 seconds while no stream is open. When a connection fails, Datastar 1.0.4 retries after
        1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 seconds and then every 30 seconds (<code>retryMaxWait</code>), ten times in all
        (<code>retryMaxCount</code>). Each retry sends the tab's signals as they are at that moment, so a context
        revived by the retry starts from the current values. A request of your own with
        <code>retry: 'error'</code> or <code>'always'</code> also retries after an HTTP error, on the same schedule
        and within the same ten attempts. The <code>retrying</code> fetch event fires only when a retry is
        scheduled. Datastar also closes the stream while the tab is hidden and opens it again when the tab is
        shown.
    </p>
    <p>
        Once the retries run out, or when a stream ends, the 15-second interval opens a new stream. That wait
        can outlast the 5-second cleanup delay, and the tab is then revived rather than reconnected to its
        context.
        Two kinds of stream end ask the tab to reconnect at once instead: a worker that stops, on a deploy or a
        reload, so another worker takes the tab, and a login that retired the session cookie the stream was
        opened with. A page that writes its own bootstrap instead of <code>via_head</code> waits for its own
        interval.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each connect renders the view again, and Datastar morphs it into the page. An attribute the browser
        set by itself, such as the <code>open</code> of a dialog opened with <code>showModal()</code>, goes
        with it unless the element lists it in <code>data-preserve-attr</code>; see
        <a href="/docs/views#preserve-attr">Attributes the browser sets</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="disconnect-detection">How a closed tab is detected</h3>
    <p>
        An open SSE stream does nothing between events. It wakes when a patch is queued for it, when its
        connection closes and when the worker stops, so <code>onClientDisconnect</code> runs about a
        millisecond after the browser closes the connection, and the tab leaves <code>getClients()</code>
        on every worker at the same moment. A stream that has sent nothing for 15 seconds writes an SSE
        comment, which browsers and Datastar ignore, so proxies that close idle connections leave it open:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withSseKeepAliveMs(15_000); // default; 0 sends no comment
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A browser that cancels one HTTP/2 stream, as it does for a hidden tab, keeps the connection open, so
        no close event fires. A server that speaks HTTP/2 checks its streams every 250 ms and ends the ones
        the browser reset. With <code>dispatch_mode</code> 1, 3 or 7 set through <code>withSwooleSettings()</code>,
        OpenSwoole has no close event, and a stream notices a closed connection at its next keep-alive
        interval. A connection that drops without closing, such as a laptop leaving Wi-Fi, stays open until
        TCP gives up on it.
    </p>
    <p>
        A tab that reconnects while its old stream is still open stays connected: the old stream ends,
        <code>onClientConnect</code> fires once, and <code>onClientDisconnect</code> fires only when the
        tab's last stream ends. A tab restored from the browser's back/forward cache after the cleanup
        delay is revived, as below, or reloads.
    </p>

    <h3 id="revival">Revival: reconnecting after cleanup, without a reload</h3>
    <p>
        When a tab is backgrounded, Datastar closes the <code>/_sse</code> stream (the right thing for
        battery and data on mobile). If it stays hidden past the cleanup delay, the context is
        destroyed. Rather than hard-reloading the returning tab, which wipes local
        (<code>_</code>-prefixed) signals the server never saw, scroll position, and focus, php-via
        <strong>revives</strong> it: the server rebuilds an equivalent context with the
        <em>same</em> context ID (so the already-loaded DOM's bindings and action URLs keep working)
        and re-seeds the signal values the client still holds and sends on reconnect. Server-owned
        TAB signals (<code>clientWritable: false</code>, or every TAB signal under
        <code>Config::withStrictTabSignals()</code>) do not take the client's copy and start from the
        handler's initial value. The tab's <a href="#tab-state">tab state</a> comes back, and
        <code>$c-&gt;input()</code> reads the page's query string again, up to 512 bytes of it.
    </p>
    <p>
        With <code>withWorkerNum()</code> above 1 the same rebuild happens when a tab's stream reconnects to a
        worker that does not hold the context, and the worker that held it hands over its TAB signal values
        while it runs; actions that land on another worker are passed to the one that holds the context. With
        one worker, the revival records live in the worker's memory, so a tab whose worker restarted reloads.
    </p>
    <p>
        This is on by default (10-minute window) and needs no application code. Because revival re-runs
        the page handler, it is not lossless: server-only state such as <code>#[Persist]</code> properties
        starts over, the handler's timers start again, and <code>onCleanup</code> and connect hooks re-fire, exactly as
        they would on a reload. Components keep their ids, since each has a namespace. Tune the window, or
        disable revival entirely (falling back to a reload on reconnect), with:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withContextTimeouts(revivalWindowMs: 600_000); // 10 minutes (default); pass 0 to disable
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The page handler of a revived tab runs behind the route's own middleware again, as on a page load, so
        an auth gate applies and the attributes it sets reach the handler. The middleware gets a GET of the
        page's URL with the cookies of the request that revives the tab, and the attributes global middleware
        set on that request: on an action, or <code>SseAwareMiddleware</code> on an SSE reconnect. When it
        answers instead of calling the handler, the tab is not revived but reloads, from an SSE reconnect or
        from an action, so the page load gets the middleware's answer, such as its redirect to a login page.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tab-state">Tab state</h2>
    <p>
        <code>$c-&gt;setTabState($key, $value)</code> keeps a server-side value of the tab that the page needs to
        rebuild itself but the browser does not hold, such as the last query result or a cursor.
        <code>$c-&gt;tabState($key, $default)</code> reads it back, also in the handler that runs again on a
        revival:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/search', function (Context $c) use ($search): void {
    $query = $c->signal('', 'query');

    $c->action(function (Context $c) use ($query, $search): void {
        $c->setTabState('results', $search->run($query->string())); // a list of strings
        $c->sync();
    }, 'search');

    $c->view(function () use ($c): string {
        $items = array_map(fn (string $r): string => '<li>' . htmlspecialchars($r) . '</li>', $c->tabState('results', []));

        return '<ul id="results">' . implode('', $items) . '</ul>';
    });
});
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Values must be serializable, never reach the browser, and are copies: change one and write it again.
        A component has keys of its own. Tab state lives as long as the context and then for the revival window.
        With one worker it stays in memory, and the revival records of destroyed tabs hold up to 64&nbsp;MiB of
        it. With more than one worker every worker reads it from the shared context directory, which holds up
        to 1&nbsp;024 serialized bytes per tab; raise that with
        <code>withContextDirectorySize(maxTabStateBytes: ...)</code>. A write from a <code>spawn()</code> task
        after the tab's context was destroyed still reaches the revived tab.
    </p>

    <h2 id="on-client-connect">onClientConnect (app-level)</h2>
    <p>
        Register an app-level callback that fires whenever <em>any</em> client's SSE connection is established.
        The callback receives the connecting client's <code>Context</code>:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->onClientConnect(function (Context $c) use ($app): void {
    // Re-render the route to update presence counters for everyone there
    $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope($c->getRoute()));
});
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="on-client-disconnect">onClientDisconnect (app-level)</h2>
    <p>
        Fires when an SSE connection closes. The client has already been removed from <code>getClients()</code>
        when this fires, so any presence count reflects the departure:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->onClientDisconnect(function (Context $c) use ($app): void {
    $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope($c->getRoute()));
});
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>These two hooks together replace a polling timer for presence features. See <a href="/docs/broadcasting">Broadcasting</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="on-cleanup">onCleanup (per-context)</h2>
    <p>
        Register cleanup logic <em>per context</em>. It fires when the context is destroyed: after the SSE
        connection closed and the cleanup delay passed, when the browser sent the close beacon, or when no stream
        connected within the connect timeout. Use it for resource cleanup specific to that tab:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->onCleanup(function (Context $c) use ($room, $app): void {
    unset(Room::$members[$room][$c->getId()]);
    $app->broadcast('room:' . $room);
});
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>A throw from a cleanup callback, or from <code>onClientConnect</code> and <code>onClientDisconnect</code>, is logged, and the other callbacks still run.</p>

    <h2 id="spawn">Background tasks</h2>
    <p>
        <code>$c-&gt;spawn($task)</code> runs per-tab work in a coroutine of its own, such as a long query that
        reports its progress; see <a href="/docs/actions#spawn">Work that outlasts the action</a>. A task keeps
        running when its context is destroyed. From then on <code>$c-&gt;isDestroyed()</code> is true and its
        syncs, patches and downloads do nothing. A stopping worker waits for running tasks, and a task that loops
        checks <code>$app-&gt;isShuttingDown()</code> to end in time. A throw from a task reaches
        <code>onError()</code> as <code>ErrorPhase::Task</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="set-interval">Recurring timers</h2>
    <p>
        <code>$c-&gt;setInterval()</code> creates an OpenSwoole timer scoped to the context.
        It fires repeatedly at the given interval (ms) and is <em>automatically cancelled</em> when the context is cleaned up:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->setInterval(function () use ($elapsed, $c): void {
    $elapsed->increment();
    $c->syncSignals(); // no action runs here, so send the value
}, 1000); // every 1 second
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>
        No need to store the timer ID or cancel it manually: it cleans up with the context. A throw from the
        callback is logged, reaches <code>onError()</code> as <code>ErrorPhase::Timer</code>, and the timer keeps
        running.
    </p>

    <h2 id="server-timers">Server-level timers</h2>
    <p>
        For timers that run regardless of who is connected (e.g. a stock price tick), use
        <code>$app-&gt;setInterval()</code>. It runs once for the whole server, on one worker even with
        <code>withWorkerNum()</code> above 1, and is cleared automatically on shutdown:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->setInterval(function () use ($app, $prices): void {
    $app->setGlobalState('price', $prices->latest());
    $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope('/ticker'));
}, 5000);
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Under the hood <code>setInterval()</code> calls <code>Timer::tick()</code> when the leader worker starts
        and clears it when that worker stops. Pass <code>everyWorker: true</code> to arm it in every worker
        instead, for work that belongs to each process. Call <code>Timer::tick()</code> from <code>onWorkerStart()</code> yourself
        when you need the timer ID for conditional logic; <code>onWorkerStart()</code> runs in every worker, so that
        timer does too. <code>$app-&gt;countClients()</code> tells a timer whether anyone is watching; see
        <a href="/docs/scopes#counting">Counting the tabs in a scope</a>.
    </p>

